# Conventional commit message <https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/>
# Format of the first line: {type}({scope})[!]: {subject}
# `type` can be one of:
# * `build`: Changes that affect only the build system or external dependencies.
# * `chore`: Routine tasks like bumping changelogs / advisories.
# * `ci`: Changes to the CI configuration files and scripts.
# * `docs`: Changes to the documentation / README only.
# * `feat`: An added feature for the user. Not a new feature for build script.
# * `fix`: A bug fix for the user. Not a fix to a build script.
# * `perf`: A code change that improves performance.
# * `refactor`: A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature, for example renaming a variable.
# * `revert`: Reverting a previous commit.
# * `style`: Changes that don't affect the meaning of the code, for example whitespace, formatting, missing semi-colons and so on.
# * `test`: Adding missing tests or correcting or refactoring existing tests.
# `scope` is optional. Use a noun which specifies a component of the product, for example `udm`, `portal` or `gitlab`.
# Append `!` to the `type` or `scope` to indicate a breaking change.
'''


